  "title": "A New Genetic Clue to How Humans Got Such Big Brains",
  "date": "04-12",
  "text": "It started with some blobs of brain-like tissue, growing in a dish. Frank Jacobs, then at the University of California at Santa Cruz, had taken stem cells from humans and monkeys, and coaxed them into forming small balls of neurons. These \"organoids\" mirror the early stages of brain development. By studying them, Jacobs could look for genes that are switched on more strongly in the growing brains of humans than in those of monkeys. And when he presented his data to his colleagues at a lab meeting, one gene grabbed everyone’s attention.",
